Q: Angular error using new input signal transform to update other property in component A: <p>Your form can be also a &quot;computedSignal&quot; in the way</p>
<pre><code>data=input&lt;any&gt;()
form=computed(()=&gt;{
    return new FormGroup({
      shiftId:new FormControl(this.data().shiftId),
      shiftGuid:new FormControl(this.data().shiftGuid)
    })
})

</code></pre>
<p>The .html</p>
<pre><code>&lt;form [formGroup]=&quot;form()&quot;&gt;
...
&lt;/form&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Use a computed while the form is &quot;inmutable&quot;. If you need a signal that you want to change, you can use a linkedLisgnal instead.</p>

Q: Instantiating an object with a function pointer, with its arguments filtered by type A: <ol>
<li>The Deduction Failure</li>
</ol>
<p>In your <code>addPattern</code>, you have:
void addPattern(void(*view)(TypeList&lt;Filter&lt;...&gt;&gt;), Types ...args)</p>
<p>The compiler tries to determine what <code>Types...</code> is by looking at both the function pointer (view) and the arguments (args...).</p>
<ul>
<li><p>From args..., it sees const char* and int.</p>
</li>
<li><p>From view, it sees a complex instruction: &quot;The result of filtering Types... must be int.&quot;</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>C++ deduction is not an equation solver. Because it can't be 100% sure what Types... should be based on the function pointer, deduction fails.</p>
<ol>
<li>The TypeList Expansion Issue</li>
</ol>
<p>Your code uses <code>TypeList&lt;int&gt;</code> as a parameter type. To the compiler, <code>TypeList&lt;int&gt;</code> is a single struct type, not a list of arguments.</p>
<ul>
<li><p>What you wanted: <code>void(int)</code></p>
</li>
<li><p>What you wrote: <code>void(TypeList&lt;int&gt;)</code></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>These are incompatible. A function expecting one integer cannot accept a TypeList object.</p>
<p>Here is a bit longish solution + added invoker</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;string&gt;
#include &lt;tuple&gt;
#include &lt;type_traits&gt;
#include &lt;utility&gt;

//  Type Filtering Logic 
template&lt;typename... Ts&gt; struct TypeList {};

template&lt;typename In, template&lt;typename&gt; class Pred, typename Out = TypeList&lt;&gt;&gt;
struct Filter;

template&lt;template&lt;typename&gt; class Pred, typename... Out&gt;
struct Filter&lt;TypeList&lt;&gt;, Pred, TypeList&lt;Out...&gt;&gt; { using type = TypeList&lt;Out...&gt;; };

//recursive engine of the filter
template&lt;typename T, typename... Rest, template&lt;typename&gt; class Pred, typename... Out&gt;
struct Filter&lt;TypeList&lt;T, Rest...&gt;, Pred, TypeList&lt;Out...&gt;&gt; {
    using type = typename std::conditional_t&lt;Pred&lt;T&gt;::value,
        Filter&lt;TypeList&lt;Rest...&gt;, Pred, TypeList&lt;Out..., T&gt;&gt;,
        Filter&lt;TypeList&lt;Rest...&gt;, Pred, TypeList&lt;Out...&gt;&gt;&gt;::type;
};

template&lt;typename T&gt; struct MakeFuncPtr;
template&lt;typename... Args&gt;
struct MakeFuncPtr&lt;TypeList&lt;Args...&gt;&gt; { using type = void(*)(Args...); };

template&lt;typename... AllArgs&gt;
class ViewPath {
    using FilteredTypes = typename Filter&lt;TypeList&lt;AllArgs...&gt;, std::is_integral&gt;::type;
    using FuncPtr = typename MakeFuncPtr&lt;FilteredTypes&gt;::type;

    FuncPtr view_func;
    std::tuple&lt;AllArgs...&gt; view_args;

    // This helper returns a 1-element tuple if T is integral, otherwise an empty tuple.
    template&lt;typename T&gt;
    auto wrap_if_integral(T&amp;&amp; val) {
        if constexpr (std::is_integral_v&lt;std::decay_t&lt;T&gt;&gt;) {
            return std::make_tuple(std::forward&lt;T&gt;(val));
        } else {
            return std::tuple&lt;&gt;{};
        }
    }

    template&lt;size_t... Is&gt;
    void invoke_helper(std::index_sequence&lt;Is...&gt;) {
        // tuple_cat joins all the 1-element and 0-element tuples into one flat list
        auto filtered_args = std::tuple_cat(wrap_if_integral(std::get&lt;Is&gt;(view_args))...);
        std::apply(view_func, filtered_args);
    }

public:
    ViewPath(FuncPtr v, AllArgs... args) 
        : view_func(v), view_args(std::make_tuple(std::move(args)...)) {}

    void call() {
        invoke_helper(std::index_sequence_for&lt;AllArgs...&gt;{});
    }
};


template&lt;typename... Types&gt;
void addPattern(typename MakeFuncPtr&lt;typename Filter&lt;TypeList&lt;Types...&gt;, std::is_integral&gt;::type&gt;::type view, Types... args) {
    ViewPath&lt;Types...&gt; vp(view, std::move(args)...);
    vp.call(); 
}

void testSingle(int a) { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Single: &quot; &lt;&lt; a &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;; }
void testMulti(int a, int b) { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Sum: &quot; &lt;&lt; (a + b) &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;; }
void testMixed(int i, char c) { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Int: &quot; &lt;&lt; i &lt;&lt; &quot;, Char: &quot; &lt;&lt; c &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;; }

int main() {
    addPattern(testSingle, &quot;Ignore me&quot;, 73);              
    addPattern(testMulti, 10, std::string(&quot;Middle&quot;), 20); 
    addPattern(testMixed, 500, &quot;Extra&quot;, 'A');             
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/hd81baeTf" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://godbolt.org/z/hd81baeTf</a></p>
<p>Notes:</p>
<p><code>std::is_integral_v&lt;char&gt;</code> is true. So 'A' passes the filter and reaches testMixed as expected here — but so would <code>bool</code>, <code>short</code>, <code>long</code> etc. If the intent is &quot;only int&quot;, the predicate should be:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;typename T&gt;
struct IsInt : std::is_same&lt;std::decay_t&lt;T&gt;, int&gt; {};
</code></pre>

Q: Force JSON.stringify() to emit NaN / Infinity or JS JSON lib that does so A: <p>If you only need infinities, recent versions of ECMAScript provide a feature that enables a certain hack which, while not in the strictest sense correct, works surprisingly well in practice.</p>
<p>Most JSON implementations (including JavaScript’s) parse numbers into IEEE 754 double-precision floats.  This means that numbers large enough not to be representable even approximately as double-precision floats will come out as infinities.  You can therefore use a replacer function and <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/JSON/rawJSON" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>JSON.rawJSON</code></a> to force infinities to be emitted as very large numeric strings that one would hope to parse back into infinities.  Like this:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const preserveInfinities = (key, val) =&gt; {
  if (val === Infinity)
    return JSON.rawJSON('9e999');
  if (val === -Infinity)
    return JSON.rawJSON('-9e999');
  return val;
}

const obj = [0, 1, Infinity];
const json = JSON.stringify(obj, preserveInfinities);

console.log('%o', json);
console.log('%o', JSON.parse(json));</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p><code>JSON.parse</code> is practically guaranteed to parse <code>9e999</code> back into ∞.  Python does that as well, even though it could theoretically parse <code>9e999</code> into an exact integer, 9 × 10<sup>999</sup>.  Although I have not surveyed implementations extensively, I have also tried Ruby (3.2.3), PHP (8.2.20), which did the same.  According to the chart at <a href="https://seriot.ch/json/parsing.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://seriot.ch/json/parsing.html</a>, most implementations parse numbers with such large exponents successfully, but the chart does not elaborate on what value results.</p>
<p>Naturally, this trick is easily defeated by parsing logic that tries to preserve big numbers exactly, like below:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const preserveIntegers = (_, val, { source }) =&gt; {
  if (/^\d+$/.test(source))
    return BigInt(source);

  let m;
  if (m = /^(\d+)(?:\.(\d+))?e(\d+)$/.exec(source)) {
    const frac = m[2] ?? '';
    const exp = BigInt(m[3]) - BigInt(frac.length);
    if (exp &gt;= 0) {
      return BigInt(`${m[1]}${frac}`) * (10n ** exp);
    }
  }

  return val;
};

console.log('%s', JSON.parse('9e999', preserveIntegers));
console.log('%s', JSON.parse('9.9e999', preserveIntegers));</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>But I would expect such implementations to be rare.  (Even then, you can counter the above with <code>`${'9'.repeat(309)}.9`</code>, but that may still not be enough for parsers that return numbers as big decimals…)</p>
<p>As for NaN, tough luck – no such workarounds here.  You will be resigned to representing them as <code>null</code> or the string <code>&quot;NaN&quot;</code>.</p>

Q: Accessing constant variables from a lambda in Visual Studio 2026 A: <p>In C++20, even if a variable is <code>constexpr</code>, if you take its address or pass an array (which decays to a pointer), it is &quot;ODR-used.&quot; This requires the variable to be captured. MSVC's C++20 compiler is particularly strict: it doesn't allow a lambda to &quot;see&quot; a local constexpr array as a pointer unless the capture is handled in a way it can track.</p>
<pre><code>void f(const void*) {}

int main() {
    constexpr float one[1] = { 1.0f };

    // Capture the pointer to the array explicitly
    [ptr = one]() {
        f(ptr);
        f(&amp;ptr[0]);
    }();
}
</code></pre>
